move-bringup (1.0.10-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * move-usb-gadget.service: makes the NCM USB-Ethernet gadget
    actually come up on CM5 (and stay reliable on CM4 across re-runs).
    Two fixes, both verified-on-hardware on CM5 Rev 1.0 + Armbian
    current 6.18.29-bcm2711:
    - New /etc/modules-load.d/move-usb-gadget.conf preloads
      libcomposite + usb_f_ncm at boot. Without these, the kernel
      hasn't registered the usb_gadget subsystem with configfs by the
      time setup-usb-network-gadget.sh runs, so the script's first
      `mkdir /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget/g1` fails with EPERM.
      Belt-and-braces modprobe in the script itself self-recovers if
      modules-load.d is somehow stripped.
    - setup-usb-network-gadget.sh start now calls stop first to clear
      partial state from a failed previous run. Without this, residual
      configs/c.1 left half-initialised by an aborted prior run
      caused `ln: failed to create symbolic link 'configs/c.1/ncm.usb0':
      No such file or directory` on every subsequent invocation.
      Stop tear-down extended to also rmdir the strings/, os_desc/,
      and interface.ncm/ subtrees that the original script left behind.
  * BUILD.md §4c: new CM5-only first-boot step documenting the
    BCM2712 EEPROM bootloader flash via `rpi-eeprom-update -f
    /tmp/pieeprom-2026-05-13.bin` (downloaded from upstream
    raspberrypi/rpi-eeprom). Without a current EEPROM the BCM2712
    firmware-mailbox rejects SET_POWER_STATE / GET_CLOCKS, leaving
    dwc2 / v3d / pispbe / raspberrypi-clk all failing with -22 (full
    cascade documented in §6d). Note: the Debian-packaged
    `rpi-eeprom-update -a` flashes a 2023-era blob from the package's
    bundled "default" channel — too old to help — so the docs steer
    users to `-f` with an explicitly-fetched recent .bin instead.

 -- Move Port <move-port@localhost>  Fri, 16 May 2026 16:00:00 +0000

move-bringup (1.0.9-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ablspi-move-cm5.dts: RTC fragment removed — turns out it can't work
    on CM5 without a hardware mod. Verified on hardware (CM5 Rev 1.0,
    7.0.6-edge-bcm2711) that the 1.0.8 overlay's RTC fragment targeted
    the wrong pin namespace: my &rp1_i2c5_44_45 pinctrl group muxed
    *RP1's internal* GPIO 44/45 as i2c5, but the Move's PCF85063 is
    wired to the SODIMM "GPIO 44/45" carrier pins which on CM5 route
    to BCM2712 SoC pads named emmc_dat5/emmc_dat6 (pin 44/45 in the
    bcm2712 pinctrl namespace, NOT the RP1 one). Per the rpi-6.12.y
    pinctrl driver (drivers/pinctrl/bcm/pinctrl-bcm2712.c) those pads
    have zero I²C alt-functions:
        PIN(44, vc_spi0, mtsif_alt, enet0, sd_card_a, mtsif_alt1,
            arm_jtag, pdm, spi_m),
        PIN(45, vc_spi0, mtsif_alt, enet0, sd_card_a, mtsif_alt1,
            arm_jtag, pdm, spi_m),
    The BCM2712 SODIMM pins that DO expose I²C (bsc_m3, alt5) are
    GPIO 40/41 — physically different SODIMM pins. So the RTC is
    hardware-unreachable on CM5 as currently wired; cutting/re-routing
    the RTC traces to GPIO 40/41 is the only software-fixable path,
    and it's a board mod. Until then the CM5 overlay ships SPI/ablspi
    only and relies on NTP for time-of-day. ablspi itself works fine
    on CM5 — /dev/ablspi0.0 appears, MoveLauncher binds the XMOS.
    Full reasoning + the mod-path is documented in the .dts header.
  * move-usb-gadget.service: two corrections so the unit cleanly
    no-ops on CM5 (where dwc2 binding is currently blocked by an
    Armbian kernel issue) instead of failing noisily.
    - ConditionPathExists=/sys/class/udc -> ConditionPathExistsGlob=
      /sys/class/udc/*. The bare PathExists check always passed
      because /sys/class/udc is created by the gadget core regardless
      of whether any UDC is registered; the glob match only fires
      when an actual UDC node (e.g. 1000480000.usb) appears.
    - Added Requires=sys-kernel-config.mount alongside the existing
      After=. After= alone only orders; without Requires=, systemd
      may start us anyway if nothing else pulled in the mount, and
      the setup script's mkdir on /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget/...
      then EPERMs out.
    Note: on CM5 the BCM2712-side dwc2 controller at /axi/usb@480000
    stays deferred indefinitely because raspberrypi-clk fails to
    probe at boot with -22, which blocks pm_genpd from powering the
    USB domain. That's a kernel/firmware-driver issue and outside
    the scope of move-bringup; the unit-condition change makes the
    gadget service tolerate it rather than parade the failure.

 -- Move Port <move-port@localhost>  Fri, 16 May 2026 14:30:00 +0000

move-bringup (1.0.8-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * ablspi-move-cm5.dts: rewrote against the actual CM5/RP1 topology
    after verifying the upstream raspberrypi/linux base DTBs
    (bcm2712-rpi-cm5-{cm4io,cm5io}.dtb). The previous overlay was a
    CM4-by-analogy stub that targeted &i2c0if / &i2c0mux / &i2c0_gpio44
    — labels that do not exist on CM5, where carrier-side GPIO/SPI/I²C
    live on the RP1 southbridge instead of the BCM2712 SoC. With those
    unresolved labels the Pi firmware would silently fail the whole
    overlay apply, taking ablspi down with it.
    The new overlay:
      - keeps &spi0 + irq-gpios=<&gpio 3 0>; both labels resolve to
        RP1 (spi@50000 / gpio@d0000), CS0 stays on physical SODIMM
        GPIO 8 per the base DTB's cs-gpios, ablspi-dkms binds via
        compatible="ablspi" unchanged.
      - targets &i2c5 (not &i2c0) for the PCF85063 RTC, because on
        CM5/RP1 the alt-function for the 44/45 pin pair is i2c5, not
        i2c0. The upstream pinctrl group `rp1_i2c5_44_45` (function=
        i2c5, pins=gpio44+gpio45, drive-strength=12mA, bias-pull-up)
        is drop-in correct — no manual mux.
      - drops the i2c0mux/i2c0if disable fragments entirely. They were
        a CM4 BCM2711 quirk; on CM5 those nodes are empty placeholders.
    Verified by compiling the .dtbo and confirming all four __fixups__
    labels (spi0, gpio, i2c5, rp1_i2c5_44_45) resolve against both
    cm4io and cm5io base DTBs.
  * config.txt: strip the family's `dtoverlay=dwc2,dr_mode=host` from
    the [cm5] block before appending our `dr_mode=otg` line. On CM5
    the BCM2712 USB controller at /axi/usb@480000 is dwc2-compatible
    ("brcm,bcm2835-usb", status=disabled), and the stock dwc2.dtbo
    applies cleanly via the &usb fixup — but the Armbian/RPi family
    default routes it host-only for [cm5], which would conflict with
    our Move/NCM-gadget OTG requirement. Mirrors the existing [cm4]
    `otg_mode=1` strip, done in both move-bringup.postinst and the
    Armbian board hook (move.csc pre_umount_final_image__zzz_move_finalise).

